a number range of 1 to 20 with no link to the suffix, so the values 1, 2 and 3 print as non-words with the wrong ending (1th, 2th, 3th), and F25 is a correct_answer and distractor family. F26 ({n}{v}) draws the suffix v from the four suffixes independently of n, so it prints wrong ordinals such as 4rd or 11st, and it is also a correct_answer family; F20 nests it as symbol2. F33 ({answer}) is a correct_answer family whose printed observed values include names and objects (Ben, egg, marble and others), so a keyed answer can be a name or an object to an ordinal question. F23 ({head} and {last}) joins a head list of people or objects to a last item drawn from the mixed pool of names and objects, so a lineup can mix people and objects and can repeat a name, and its length is 7 to 10 items with no link to the symbol. F14 and F24 print symbols 8th, 9th and 10th (F14: 8th, 9th, 10th; F24: 8th) on lineups that can have only 7 items (the F27 head plus one last), so the stated place can be absent. F24 also says Who for object lineups, and F14 prints The before a name list. F20 can put a name or object in the place slot (symbol2 nests F34 and F33 as well as F26), giving phrases such as place or egg place. Clause verdicts are PRODUCIBLE for all four clauses, from families F13, F15, F17 and F04. Not failing: F34 offers names and objects as distractors, which is a mixed-type option set but not a wrong statement; F16, F12 and F22 print a word and a symbol with no declared link, but the observed word lists exclude the same values as the symbol lists, so the pairing is not shown to be wrong.\"}",
